# Copy to `.env` and adjust if needed. The defaults match docker-compose.yml.
#
# The one required var to run silo. Points at the Postgres from `pnpm db:up`.
DATABASE_URL=postgres://silo:silo@localhost:5432/silo

# Optional: a separate connection string for the pg-boss job queue pool.
# Falls back to DATABASE_URL when unset — leave commented for local dev.
# WORKER_DATABASE_URL=postgres://silo:silo@localhost:5432/silo

# Optional: the port @silo/api's Hono server listens on (pnpm --filter
# @silo/api dev/start). Defaults to 8787 when unset. The API is served at
# http://localhost:PORT (e.g. http://localhost:8787/health).
# PORT=8787

# Optional: the address @silo/api binds to. Defaults to loopback (127.0.0.1) —
# the API has NO auth, so binding wider exposes the whole store to your network.
# Set this only on a trusted, isolated network; it prints a loud warning.
# HOST=0.0.0.0

# Optional (but REQUIRED to use POST /api/ingest): the bearer token that
# gates BOTH:
#   1. the trusted ingest seam a local ingest tool (e.g. the future
#      `silo ingest x` CLI command) uses to capture a link WITH pre-extracted
#      sourceData (e.g. a Field Theory twitter bookmark) — POST /api/ingest is
#      closed by default: with this unset, EVERY request to /api/ingest gets
#      401, even from localhost.
#   2. OPTIONALLY, every other /api/* route (GET /health is exempt) — WHEN
#      this is set, every other route also requires a matching
#      `Authorization: Bearer <token>` header (401 without it). WHEN unset,
#      every other route has NO auth at all (today's default localhost
#      behavior — see docs/rules/api-hono.md's "Auth (there is none)"), only
#      /api/ingest is gated.
# This is the MACHINE credential: the Chrome/Raycast extensions and the MCP
# server authenticate with `Authorization: Bearer <SILO_API_TOKEN>`. Humans
# logging into the WEB UI use SILO_APP_PASSWORD instead (below) — the two are
# separate on purpose (a human shouldn't have to type a machine token, and
# rotating one shouldn't rotate the other). Setting EITHER SILO_API_TOKEN or
# SILO_APP_PASSWORD turns the general /api/* gate on.
# Pick a long random value (e.g. `openssl rand -hex 32`) — it is a real
# credential, keep it out of source control and shell history.
# SILO_API_TOKEN=

# Optional: the WEB-UI login password (web-auth cookie upgrade,
# docs/superpowers/specs/2026-07-11-web-auth-cookie-upgrade.md). UNSET (default
# localhost dev) -> the web UI shows NO login screen and works exactly as
# before. SET -> the web UI shows a login screen; a human enters this password
# once and the server exchanges it for a signed, HTTP-only `silo_session`
# cookie (never a token held in the browser). The gate then accepts EITHER that
# cookie (browser) OR the `SILO_API_TOKEN` bearer (extensions/MCP), so setting
# a web password never affects the extensions.
# SECURITY: this password doubles as the HMAC signing key for the session
# cookie when SILO_SESSION_SECRET (below) is unset, so pick a STRONG value —
# at least 16 characters, ideally `openssl rand -hex 32`. A weak/guessable
# password (e.g. "admin") is the one real risk here: login is not rate-limited
# (single-user tool), so a weak password can be guessed online. Keep it out of
# source control and shell history.
# SILO_APP_PASSWORD=

# Optional: an independent HMAC secret for signing the `silo_session` cookie.
# UNSET -> the cookie is signed with SILO_APP_PASSWORD itself (a single-secret
# deployment Just Works). SET -> the signing key is decoupled from the login
# password, so rotating the signing secret invalidates every outstanding
# session WITHOUT changing the password users type (and lets you use a strong
# random signing key even if the password is more memorable). Use
# `openssl rand -hex 32`. Rotating this (or SILO_APP_PASSWORD) is also how you
# force-log-out every session — the stateless cookie has no server-side
# revocation list (see the spec's "Non-goals").
# SILO_SESSION_SECRET=

# Optional: expose the MCP server over HTTP (in addition to stdio) on this
# port, from the turnkey `@silo/app` process. UNSET -> MCP is stdio-only
# (today's default: a subprocess an MCP client launches, no network surface).
# SET -> the turnkey ALSO listens for MCP over HTTP at
# http://127.0.0.1:PORT/mcp, so a networked agent connects by inserting the
# API key. This listener is ALWAYS-CLOSED: it requires SILO_API_TOKEN (above)
# and every request must send `Authorization: Bearer <SILO_API_TOKEN>`. If
# you set this port but leave SILO_API_TOKEN unset, the process REFUSES to
# open the listener (loud stderr warning) rather than expose an
# unauthenticated networked MCP endpoint — stdio MCP still works. Binds
# loopback (127.0.0.1) only by default (see SILO_MCP_HTTP_HOST below to bind
# wider in a container).
# SILO_MCP_HTTP_PORT=8788

# Optional (Docker/hosted MCP container — see docs/deploy.md): the host the
# HTTP MCP listener binds. Defaults to 127.0.0.1 (loopback). In a container it
# must bind 0.0.0.0 so the reverse proxy can reach it. Only the standalone MCP
# entrypoint (packages/app/src/mcp-http-main.ts) reads this.
# SILO_MCP_HTTP_HOST=0.0.0.0

# Optional (hosted MCP behind a reverse proxy — REQUIRED there): comma-separated
# PUBLIC hostname(s) the MCP listener is served under, added VERBATIM (no port)
# to the MCP SDK's DNS-rebinding allowlist. Behind Traefik the incoming Host
# header is your public hostname with no port (e.g. mcp.silo.example.com on
# :443), which the listener's default 127.0.0.1:<port> allowlist would REJECT
# (403). List your mcp subdomain(s) here so proxied requests are trusted.
#   SILO_MCP_ALLOWED_HOSTS=mcp.silo.example.com
# SILO_MCP_ALLOWED_HOSTS=

# Optional (hosted — the "Copy config" MCP URL): the exact public MCP endpoint
# the web UI's "Copy config" button hands to agents. UNSET -> the web derives
# https://mcp.<web-hostname>/mcp (works if you use the silo.<domain> /
# mcp.silo.<domain> naming), falling back to http://127.0.0.1:8788/mcp on
# localhost. SET -> used verbatim (for a bare domain or a non-standard MCP
# subdomain). See docs/deploy.md.
# ALSO the RFC 8707 OAuth resource identifier for the MCP OAuth flow (see
# docs/methods/mcp-oauth.md) — set on BOTH the api and mcp containers, and
# they MUST agree: both derive the same canonical resource string
# (https://mcp.<domain>/mcp) from this value, and a token minted for one
# value is rejected against another.
#   SILO_PUBLIC_MCP_URL=https://mcp.silo.example.com/mcp
# SILO_PUBLIC_MCP_URL=

# Optional (hosted — MCP OAuth): the public origin of the api container (e.g.
# https://silo.example.com) — the OAuth issuer. UNSET -> the issuer is
# derived from the incoming request's Host header (works if the api origin
# is reached consistently through one hostname). SET -> used verbatim as
# `issuer` in /.well-known/oauth-authorization-server, and advertised as the
# `authorization_servers` entry the mcp container returns from
# /.well-known/oauth-protected-resource (mcp reads this var too — set it on
# both containers, or at least on mcp, since that's the one that needs it to
# point Claude/ChatGPT back at the right auth server). See
# docs/methods/mcp-oauth.md.
#   SILO_PUBLIC_API_URL=https://silo.example.com
# SILO_PUBLIC_API_URL=

# Optional: comma-separated list of origins allowed to call /api/* cross-
# origin (CORS). UNSET -> safe localhost defaults (http://localhost:5173 —
# the web UI's Vite dev server — and http://localhost:8787 — this API's own
# default port). SET -> exactly the listed origins, nothing implicit added.
# A production deployment should list every trusted origin explicitly, e.g.
# the packed Chrome extension's id and the deployed web UI's real origin:
#   SILO_ALLOWED_ORIGINS=chrome-extension://<extension-id>,https://silo.example.com
# NEVER set this to "*" — the allowlist IS the security boundary for this
# API (which otherwise has no auth by default); a wildcard would let any
# web page read/write/delete the whole store via the visitor's browser.
# SILO_ALLOWED_ORIGINS=

# Optional: the worker's scheduled maintenance jobs (packages/worker/src/jobs).
# The trash-purge cycle is also settable per-user in the web Settings screen;
# this env var is the worker-side default/override — an unset or malformed
# value falls back to the documented default below.
#
# Trash retention window in days for the daily `purge-trash` job. Defaults to 30.
# SILO_TRASH_PURGE_DAYS=30
#
# Minutes a link may sit at capture_status='enriching' before the ~5-minute
# `sweep-enriching` job considers it stranded and re-enqueues it. Defaults to 15.
# SILO_ENRICHING_STALE_MINUTES=15
